---
import ToolPageLayout from '../layouts/ToolPageLayout.astro';
import TakeHomeCalculator from '../islands/TakeHomeCalculator';
import taxRules from '../data/tax/2026.json';

const meta = taxRules.meta;
const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'How do I compute my take-home pay in the Philippines?',
    a: 'Start with your monthly gross salary, deduct your employee shares of SSS (5% of your Monthly Salary Credit), PhilHealth (2.5% of basic salary, floor ₱10,000 / ceiling ₱100,000) and Pag-IBIG (2% of fund salary, max ₱200), then apply the BIR withholding tax table to the remainder. What is left is your take-home (net) pay.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How much is the take-home pay for a ₱30,000 salary in 2026?',
    a: 'At ₱30,000 gross monthly: SSS ₱1,500 + PhilHealth ₱750 + Pag-IBIG ₱200 = ₱2,450 in contributions. Taxable income is ₱27,550, giving ₱1,007.55 withholding tax. Estimated take-home pay: ₱26,542.45.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What monthly salary is tax-free in the Philippines?',
    a: 'Under the TRAIN law, annual taxable income of ₱250,000 or less is exempt — that is monthly taxable income up to ₱20,833. Because mandatory contributions are deducted first, a gross salary of roughly ₱23,000–₱23,500 per month can still result in zero withholding tax.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Are SSS, PhilHealth and Pag-IBIG deducted before or after tax?',
    a: 'Before. Your mandatory contributions (employee shares only) are excluded from taxable compensation, so the withholding tax is computed on gross salary minus those contributions.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is the 13th month pay included in this computation?',
    a: 'No — this calculator estimates your regular monthly net pay. The 13th month pay is a separate annual benefit, tax-exempt up to ₱90,000 combined with other benefits. Use the 13th Month Pay Calculator for that.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the standard salary computation in the Philippines?',
    a: 'Philippine payroll computes net salary in a fixed order: gross monthly salary, minus the employee shares of SSS, PhilHealth and Pag-IBIG, then BIR withholding tax on the remainder using the monthly table under the TRAIN law. Premiums such as overtime, night differential and holiday pay form part of gross pay; the 13th month pay is a separate annual computation. The calculator above follows this exact order.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I compute my salary with absences?',
    a: 'Convert your monthly salary to a daily rate — monthly × 12 ÷ 261 for a five-day week or ÷ 313 for a six-day week — and deduct one daily rate per unpaid day (and the hourly rate, daily ÷ 8, for late or undertime hours). Run the reduced gross through the calculator: at ₱20,000 with 2 days absent on a five-day week, gross becomes ₱20,000 − ₱1,839.08 = ₱18,160.92 before contributions and tax.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does this salary calculator work for government employees?',
    a: 'Not exactly. Government employees contribute to GSIS instead of SSS — 9% of monthly compensation as the personal share, with 12% from the agency — so the SSS line here does not apply. PhilHealth, Pag-IBIG and the BIR withholding table are the same. Use the result as a guide and replace the SSS deduction with your GSIS premium.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Magkano ang daily rate ko kung ₱20,000 ang monthly na sahod?',
    a: 'Depende sa factor ng kumpanya mo: ₱20,000 × 12 ÷ 261 = ₱919.54 kung five-day week, o ₱20,000 × 12 ÷ 313 = ₱766.77 kung six-day week na walang bayad sa rest day. Hatiin sa 8 para sa hourly rate: ₱114.94 o ₱95.85.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Paano gamitin ito bilang suweldo calculator?',
    a: 'Ilagay ang gross monthly na suweldo mo, at ikukuwenta ng calculator ang kaltas sa SSS, PhilHealth at Pag-IBIG at ang withholding tax ayon sa 2026 na talahanayan — ang lalabas ay ang net na suweldo o take-home pay. Kung may absent, overtime o night differential, ayusin muna ang gross (bawasin ang absent, idagdag ang premium) bago ilagay.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Magkano ang take-home pay sa ₱25,000 na sahod?',
    a: 'Deductions: SSS ₱1,250 (5% of the ₱25,000 MSC), PhilHealth ₱625 (2.5%), Pag-IBIG ₱200 — total ₱2,075. Taxable income is ₱22,925, so withholding tax is 15% of the excess over ₱20,833 = ₱313.80. Take-home pay: ₱25,000 − ₱2,075 − ₱313.80 = ₱22,611.20.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Bakit walang tax ang ₱20,000 na sahod?',
    a: 'Because withholding tax is computed after the contributions: ₱20,000 minus SSS ₱1,000, PhilHealth ₱500 and Pag-IBIG ₱200 leaves ₱18,300, which is below the ₱20,833 monthly threshold (₱250,000 a year) where tax starts under the TRAIN table. Take-home pay is therefore ₱18,300.',
  },
];
---

<ToolPageLayout
  title="Take-Home Pay Calculator Philippines 2026 – Net Salary After Tax"
  description="Compute your monthly take-home pay in the Philippines: gross salary minus SSS, PhilHealth, Pag-IBIG and BIR withholding tax, every deduction itemized."
  path="/take-home-pay-calculator/"
  h1="Take-Home Pay Calculator Philippines"
  faqs={faqs}
  related={['sssCalculator', 'philhealth', 'pagibig', 'thirteenth']}
>
  <Fragment slot="answer">
    <p>
      Your take-home pay is your <strong>gross monthly salary minus four deductions</strong>: your
      employee shares of SSS, PhilHealth and Pag-IBIG contributions, and the BIR withholding tax
      computed on what remains. This calculator follows the standard salary computation used by
      Philippine payroll — the exact 2026 schedules for all four deductions — and itemizes every
      line.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="calculator">
    <TakeHomeCalculator client:idle />
  </Fragment>

  <h2>How the Salary Computation Works</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/sss-contribution-calculator/">SSS</a>:</strong> 5% of your Monthly Salary
      Credit (MSC ₱5,000–₱35,000), per the official schedule — max ₱1,750.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/philhealth-contribution/">PhilHealth</a>:</strong> half of the 5% premium
      on basic salary (floor ₱10,000 / ceiling ₱100,000) — you pay 2.5%, max ₱2,500.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/pagibig-contribution/">Pag-IBIG</a>:</strong> 2% of fund salary capped at
      ₱10,000 — max ₱200.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Withholding tax:</strong> BIR's monthly table (TRAIN law, 2023-onwards schedule)
      applied to gross salary minus the three contributions above.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>BIR Monthly Withholding Tax Table (2023 onwards, in force 2026)</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Monthly Taxable Income</th><th>Withholding Tax</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>₱20,833 and below</td><td>₱0</td></tr>
        <tr><td>₱20,833 – ₱33,332</td><td>15% of excess over ₱20,833</td></tr>
        <tr><td>₱33,333 – ₱66,666</td><td>₱1,875 + 20% of excess over ₱33,333</td></tr>
        <tr><td>₱66,667 – ₱166,666</td><td>₱8,541.80 + 25% of excess over ₱66,667</td></tr>
        <tr><td>₱166,667 – ₱666,666</td><td>₱33,541.80 + 30% of excess over ₱166,667</td></tr>
        <tr><td>₱666,667 and above</td><td>₱183,541.80 + 35% of excess over ₱666,667</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Example Computations</h2>
  <div class="example-card">
    <p><strong>Example 1 — ₱20,000 gross:</strong> SSS ₱1,000 + PhilHealth ₱500 + Pag-IBIG ₱200 =
    ₱1,700. Taxable ₱18,300 → tax ₱0. Take-home: <strong>₱18,300</strong>.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="example-card">
    <p><strong>Example 2 — ₱30,000 gross:</strong> contributions ₱2,450; taxable ₱27,550 → tax
    ₱1,007.55. Take-home: <strong>₱26,542.45</strong>.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="example-card">
    <p><strong>Example 3 — ₱60,000 gross:</strong> SSS ₱1,750 (capped) + PhilHealth ₱1,500 +
    Pag-IBIG ₱200 = ₱3,450. Taxable ₱56,550 → tax ₱1,875 + 20% × ₱23,217 = ₱6,518.40. Take-home:
    <strong>₱50,031.60</strong>.</p>
  </div>
  <p>
    The four BIR withholding tables (monthly, semi-monthly, weekly, daily), the annual rates and the year-end adjustment are on
    the <a href="/income-tax-calculator/">Income Tax Calculator</a>; for freelance or side income see the
    <a href="/freelancer-tax-calculator/">Freelancer Tax Calculator (8% vs graduated)</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Semi-Monthly, Daily and Hourly Equivalents</h2>
  <p>
    Payroll usually pays twice a month, so each cutoff is roughly <strong>half of the monthly
    take-home</strong>. Employers may deduct the full SSS, PhilHealth and Pag-IBIG contributions on
    one cutoff or split them; the monthly total is the same either way. To convert a monthly
    salary to a daily rate, DOLE's formula is <strong>monthly rate × 12 ÷ factor</strong>, where
    the factor is the number of paid days in a year: 365 if you are paid every day including rest
    days and holidays, 313 for a six-day work week not paid on rest days, or 261 for a five-day
    week. Hourly rate = daily rate ÷ 8.
  </p>
  <div class="example-card">
    <p><strong>Example — ₱30,000 monthly, five-day week:</strong> daily rate = ₱30,000 × 12 ÷ 261
    = <strong>₱1,379.31</strong>; hourly rate = ₱1,379.31 ÷ 8 = <strong>₱172.41</strong>.
    Semi-monthly take-home ≈ ₱26,542.45 ÷ 2 = <strong>₱13,271.23</strong> per cutoff.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Salary Computation With Absences, Overtime and Night Differential</h2>
  <p>
    The calculator takes one gross monthly figure. To match a real payslip, adjust the gross
    before you enter it: <strong>absences and undertime</strong> are deducted at your daily or
    hourly rate (daily rate = monthly × 12 ÷ factor, hourly = daily ÷ 8), while
    <strong>overtime, night differential and holiday premiums</strong> are added on top of basic
    pay. Premiums are taxable compensation, so they raise the withholding tax; SSS uses your total
    compensation for the month to find the salary credit, PhilHealth uses basic salary, and
    Pag-IBIG stays capped at ₱200. Compute the premiums first with the
    <a href="/overtime-pay-calculator/">Overtime</a>,
    <a href="/night-differential-calculator/">Night Differential</a> and
    <a href="/holiday-pay-calculator/">Holiday Pay</a> calculators, then enter the adjusted gross
    here.
  </p>
  <div class="example-card">
    <p><strong>Example — ₱20,000 monthly, five-day week, 2 days absent, 10 OT hours:</strong>
    daily rate ₱20,000 × 12 ÷ 261 = ₱919.54; absences −₱1,839.08; OT ₱919.54 ÷ 8 × 125% × 10 =
    ₱1,436.78. Adjusted gross = ₱20,000 − ₱1,839.08 + ₱1,436.78 = <strong>₱19,597.70</strong>,
    which you then run through the calculator above.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Who This Applies To</h2>
  <p>
    Private-sector employees paid monthly, with mandatory contributions deducted through payroll.
    It assumes the standard graduated withholding table — not the 8% flat option for self-employed
    taxpayers, government GSIS deductions, de minimis benefits, or company-specific deductions
    (loans, HMO top-ups, tardiness). Your payslip may differ where those apply; treat this result
    as the statutory baseline.
  </p>

  <h2>Official Source</h2>
  <p>
    Withholding tax: <strong>{meta.rule_version}</strong> —
    <a href={meta.official_source_url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{meta.official_source_label}</a>.
    Contribution schedules: SSS Circular 2024-006, PhilHealth Advisory 2026-0042, HDMF Circular
    460 — each linked on its own calculator page. Last verified
    {new Date(meta.last_verified + 'T00:00:00').toLocaleDateString('en-PH', { month: 'long', day: 'numeric', year: 'numeric' })}.
  </p>
</ToolPageLayout>
